                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is 18:40 on a Friday. Casualties are self-presenting and your <strong>EPR and bed-management systems are down</strong> — you cannot see the hospital in real time. Reports from ED, ambulance command, IT and paper ward sit-reps conflict. Your first job is sensemaking: build a workable picture from those who can <em>see the ground</em>, separate what you know from what you assume, and name the two or three decisions that cannot wait. The trap is freezing for certainty, or routing everything through a blind central cell. With ~50 free beds against a 560-patient surge, decide how much authority to push to the edge now.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Allocate scarce response resources by rule`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Every line is over-subscribed: <strong>6 surge theatres/bays, 40 redeployable staff, 12 critical-care beds and £1.2M contingency</strong> against trauma surge, decontamination, urgent non-incident care and staff welfare. Every allocation starves something. Under scarcity you need an <em>explicit prioritisation rule</em>, not a first-come reaction — and a deliberately protected reserve. The surge runs 48 hours; if you burn the contingency and the full staff pool in these first six hours, you have nothing left for the night-two collapse.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Decide: centralized vs distributed leadership`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `This is the Spyridonidis core. Design the decision-rights model for the next 24 hours. With the centre blind, <strong>holding authority at the executive cell is a bottleneck, not control</strong> — decisions an ED registrar could make in two minutes take forty. But distribution is not abdication: it needs a single, clear <em>commander's intent</em> every local leader decides within, and a way for two sites to coordinate without the EPR. Choose deliberately between centralized command, mission command, or a calibrated mix — and justify it against the information reality.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Frame the message & defend the response`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Your distributed model only switches on through the <strong>message to the frontline</strong>. A frightened, exhausted night shift will act decisively only if the framing is honest about the threat, clear about their authority within the intent, and confidence-building. “Await instructions” paralyses; “use your judgement” with no frame creates chaos. Then defend the whole response to regional command at hour 48 — showing the model, allocation and message cohere and flow is stabilising. The trap is <em>optimising for the review rather than the ground</em>: if you win the briefing but lose the frontline, the numbers will not be real.`,

