                    coach_default:          `Command decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_ground:        `<strong>That is sensemaking done right.</strong> With the EPR down, the only real-time picture lives with the people who can see the wards. Runners and site huddles give you a working account to act on — imperfect, but actionable now, which beats a perfect picture you will never get.`,
                    coach_r1_single:        `<strong>One central paper dashboard sounds orderly</strong> — but it routes a blind centre's view through a single choke point and lags the ground badly. You can collate centrally, yet the live picture still has to come from those who can see the wards.`,
                    coach_r1_wait:          `<strong>Waiting for the EPR is waiting for certainty you will not get.</strong> The casualties are arriving now. Sensemaking means acting on a workable, partial account — freeze for perfect information and the surge overwhelms you before IT restores a thing.`,
                    coach_r1_assume:        `<strong>Pre-outage figures are already fiction.</strong> Occupancy moved the moment the surge began. Acting on stale numbers as if they were live is how you send patients to beds that no longer exist.`,
                    coach_r1_audit:         `<strong>The hour-48 paperwork is not your six-hour risk.</strong> Naming the audit as the priority tonight tells your team you are managing the review, not the crisis. The real risk is operational — flow, exit, and a thin workforce.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You pulled the picture from the ground AND named exit block as the killer — with no flow out, surge capacity fills and stops, no matter how many beds you open. That is the decision that cannot wait.`,
                    coach_r1_central:       `<strong>You are holding the urgent calls at a blind centre.</strong> Decisions an ED registrar could make in two minutes will take forty routed up to a cell that cannot see the ward. Push more authority to those who can actually see the patients.`,
                    coach_r2_clinical:      `<strong>A published clinical-priority rule is exactly right.</strong> Every site lead now knows how the next critical-care bed is decided, so allocation runs on logic instead of volume — and a protected reserve survives for the night-two collapse.`,
                    coach_r2_firstcome:     `<strong>First-come, first-served is a reaction, not a rule.</strong> The surge theatres go to whoever called first while trauma queues behind them. Under scarcity you need an explicit prioritisation rule that decides who gets the next bed — and a reserve.`,
                    coach_r2_loudest:       `<strong>You have rewarded the loudest voice.</strong> Capacity flows to whichever consultant escalates hardest, not to clinical need, and your quieter, sicker patients lose out. That is not triage — it is volume management.`,
                    coach_r2_equal:         `<strong>Splitting everything equally ignores the demand.</strong> One site is taking the trauma surge and the other is not — equal shares strand capacity where it is not needed and starve the site that is drowning. Allocate to need, by rule.`,
                    coach_r2_paced:         `<strong>Rule-based and paced — well held.</strong> You rationed by clinical priority, fed the dominant trauma demand, and kept a real reserve of staff and money back for night two. That is crisis leadership that lasts the full 48 hours, not just the first six.`,
                    coach_r2_frontload:     `<strong>It is hour six and you have nothing left.</strong> You have spent the contingency and redeployed almost everyone — and the surge runs another 36 hours. Crisis response is paced. What do you hold for the night-two collapse, and the staff you still need on Sunday?`,
                    coach_r2_thinreserve:   `<strong>Good rule — thin reserve.</strong> Your prioritisation is sound, but you have left little staff or money for night two. Pull something back: a 48-hour event punishes a response that spends itself in the first six hours.`,
                    coach_r2_starvetrauma:  `<strong>You have starved the trauma surge.</strong> Severnvale is the regional trauma centre — resus and theatres are the dominant demand tonight. Under-staffing them while other lines sit comfortable is exactly the allocation that lets flow collapse.`,
                    coach_r2_droppedcare:   `<strong>Urgent non-incident care has been abandoned.</strong> The heart attacks and strokes that always arrive have not stopped because there is a major incident. Strip that line bare and you trade one crisis for another — and your staff see it.`,
                    coach_r2_underspend:    `<strong>You have under-resourced the response.</strong> A regional surge centre cannot meet a 560-patient surge on a skeleton deployment and almost no contingency. Holding a reserve is wise; holding back the whole response is not.`,
                    coach_r3_distributed:   `<strong>Mission command is the right instinct here.</strong> With the centre blind, authority belongs with ward, ED and site leaders who can see the patients. Now make sure they are deciding within one clear intent, or distribution turns into duplication.`,
                    coach_r3_calibrated:    `<strong>A calibrated mix is defensible.</strong> Strategic calls central, operational calls at the edge — coherent, provided the boundary is explicit and the intent is clear. Lean a little further toward the edge while the EPR is down and the centre cannot see.`,
                    coach_r3_central:       `<strong>This is the headline failure.</strong> You have routed every bed and theatre decision up to a cell that cannot see the wards. Decisions are now taking forty minutes the ED registrar could make in two. When the centre cannot see the ground, holding authority at the centre is not control — it is a bottleneck.`,
                    coach_r3_compete:       `<strong>Independent sites with no shared cadence will collide.</strong> Both sites are now solving the same problem twice and competing for the same agency nurses. Distributed leadership still needs coordination — a shared picture and a sit-rep rhythm.`,
                    coach_r3_micro:         `<strong>Phoning each ward on demand is micromanagement in disguise.</strong> It interrupts the people doing the work and still leaves the centre with a partial, lagging picture. A timed liaison cadence respects the ward and feeds you a coherent view.`,
                    coach_r3_nointent:      `<strong>You have distributed authority with no intent.</strong> You told every ward and site leader to use their judgement, but gave them no shared frame — so the two sites solve the same problem twice and compete for the same nurses. What is the one-line intent every local leader is deciding within?`,
                    coach_r3_missioncommand:`<strong>That is mission command done properly.</strong> Authority sits with those who can see the ground, bounded by a clear commander's intent, with a liaison cadence keeping two sites coherent. Fast where it needs to be fast, coordinated where it needs to cohere.`,
                    coach_r4_empower:       `<strong>That message switches the distributed model on.</strong> Honest about the threat, explicit that you trust their judgement within the intent, and clear they are not alone — that is the sentence that lets a charge nurse act with confidence at 2am instead of waiting for a call.`,
                    coach_r4_await:         `<strong>“Business as usual, await instructions” paralyses.</strong> Your night staff are on paper, frightened and outnumbered — that message reads as either denial or an order to stop thinking. It quietly switches your distributed model back off.`,
                    coach_r4_judgement:     `<strong>“Use your judgement” with no frame is abdication.</strong> You have granted permission without direction, so confident people pull in different directions and the two sites diverge. Empowerment needs the intent attached, or it becomes chaos.`,
                    coach_r4_nomessage:     `<strong>No message to the frontline is itself a message.</strong> Silence at the top, on a frightened paper-bound night shift, reads as abandonment — and the briefing you polish instead cannot stabilise a single ward. The message is the leadership act, not the afterthought.`,
                    coach_r4_ground:        `<strong>You optimised for the ground.</strong> Stabilise the patients and keep the frontline with you and the hour-48 numbers are real, not staged. That is where legitimacy actually comes from — the wards, not the upward narrative.`,
                    coach_r4_review:        `<strong>You have built a briefing that will look excellent.</strong> But if two wards are unsafe and the staff do not trust the plan, the numbers you present at hour 48 are not real. Manage the review and lose the frontline, and the whole thing unravels the moment regional command looks closely.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Leading the review with the ransomware excuse reads as deflection.</strong> Regional command knows about the outage — what they are judging is whether your command was coherent despite it. Show the response, not the alibi.`,

